3: Goal: In the production messaging drawer, no message a user sends is lost or duplicated across network and Realtime 
outages, reloads, several tabs, account changes, and several devices; typing, unread/read state, and @mention 
notifications stay live and correct; and messaging health is observable through redacted logs — without regressing 
existing messaging.
4: 
5: Status: Draft v2 — revised after adversarial review r2 (`reviews/sol-review-spec-r2.md`); pending re-review and 
owner approval. Interview rounds 1–2, confirmation, and post-review owner decisions D1–D4 recorded in `state.md` 
(Verdict: Ready).
6: 
7: ## 1. Summary
8: 
9: The Virtual Office messaging drawer (`MessagingDrawer` → `MessageFeed` → `MessageComposer` / `MessageItem`, state 
in `MessagingContext`, data in `useMessages` / `useConversations`, Realtime in `useMessageSubscription` / 
`useConversationRealtime`, typing in `useConversationPresence`) was completed for timeline features in Phase 4. Phase 
4 also delivered a failed send that keeps text/reply/files with a single-message retry (`clientMessageId` idempotency 
in `POST /api/messages/create`), a Realtime catch-up after reconnect, and opt-in desktop notifications for DM/group 
messages. This phase adds: a durable per-user outbox for text messages with safe cross-tab and cross-account behavior; 
a connection banner; application-level recovery and health for every messaging Realtime stream with polling fallback; 
a reliable, named, members-only typing indicator; live cross-device unread/read sync; @mentions with desktop 
notifications (including rooms); messaging health metrics as redacted server logs; and two Phase 4 database follow-ups 
plus restoring the open conversation after reload.
10: 
11: ## 2. Problem statement
12: 
13: Workspace members using the chat on unstable networks or sleeping laptops lose work and trust: a send that fails 
must be retried by hand, a reload or closed tab discards it (the idempotency key lives only in component memory), and 
nothing tells the user the chat is disconnected. Realtime recovery is incomplete: the SDK retries errored/timed-out 
channels, but the application only tracks and catches up the messages channel, has no recovery for terminal closures 
of the conversation-list channel, and has no fallback, so new messages and unread counts can silently stop updating. 
The typing indicator disappears after ~4 s of continuous typing and is broadcast on a public channel. Reading on one 
device leaves stale unread badges on the user's other devices. Nobody can mention someone, so room conversations 
(which never notify) cannot reach a specific person. Nobody can tell in production whether messaging is healthy.
14: 
15: ## 3. Goals and success metrics
16: 
17: ### Goals
18: - G1: A text message the user sends is never silently lost: it is delivered, pending, or visibly failed with an 
action.
19: - G2: No message is ever stored or shown twice because of automatic resends, several tabs, lost responses, or 
account changes; a message is never stored under a different sender.
20: - G3: Messaging recovers from network and Realtime outages without user action and keeps updating through polling 
while Realtime is down.
21: - G4: Typing indicators are reliable, named, and visible only to conversation members.
22: - G5: Unread/read state and own sent messages stay in sync live (≤ 5 s) across the same user's tabs and devices.
23: - G6: A user can @mention conversation members, and the mentioned person is notified on the desktop, including in 
rooms.
24: - G7: Messaging health (latency, delivery, connection) is observable in technical logs without exposing content.
25: - G8: No existing messaging behavior regresses; every database change reaches the online target in a controlled, 
verified way at phase end.
26: 
27: ### Success metrics
28: - With ≤ 10 pending messages and create quota available, all pending messages are delivered within 10 s after 
connectivity returns, without any click, in their original order, each exactly once; with more, delivery continues 
automatically within the server rate limit (D1).
29: - With Realtime down and HTTP working, other members' new messages appear within 45 s of the outage start, and 
within 15 s (plus request time) of being sent once polling is active.
30: - Cross-device/tab sync of unread counts, read state, and own sent messages within 5 s (D4).
31: - Each flow is covered by a passing Playwright E2E test (two accounts, and two tabs / two devices where relevant) 
that passed 3 consecutive runs in Chrome and Edge.
32: - The existing messaging regression E2E suite passes after each slice.
33: - Every migration of the phase is applied to the online target `vhabpcoyypobgasacsko` at phase end with explicit 
owner authorization, catalog readback, and a runtime smoke check.
34: - Owner final test (new app against the online database) accepts the phase.
35: 
36: ## 4. Users and stakeholders
37: 
38: ### Primary users
39: - All workspace members who use the chat in direct, group, and room conversations (members of 
`conversation_members`), as sender, recipient, or mentioned user.
40: 
41: ### Secondary users
42: - The same user on several tabs of one browser and on several devices/browsers at once; several users sharing one 
browser.
43: - The owner/operator, who reads the messaging metric logs.
44: 
45: ### Stakeholders
46: - Owner (Giuliano): approves the SPEC, performs the final test, authorizes the online migrations at phase end, and 
deploys the application.
47: 
48: ## 5. Scope
49: 
50: ### In scope
51: - Durable outbox (IndexedDB, per application user, per browser) for text messages and replies, written before 
dispatch; pending/sending/unknown/failed/expired states; cancel; ordered automatic drain; cross-tab claim and 
recovery; identity fencing; rejection, rate-limit, and 24 h expiry handling; session-end and sign-out rules.
52: - Server support: sender fencing on create; read-only lookup of a stored message by `clientMessageId`; replays of 
already-stored messages not consuming the create quota; mentions persisted atomically with the message.
53: - Connection banner in the drawer.
54: - Application-level recovery, health, and catch-up for every messaging Realtime stream 
(messages/receipts/reactions, conversation list, typing of the open conversation).
55: - Polling fallback after > 30 s of Realtime outage, while the tab is visible.
56: - Typing indicator: continuous display, names, DM/group/room, private members-only channel, own sessions excluded, 
expiry.
57: - Live (≤ 5 s) cross-device/tab sync of unread counters, read state, and own sent messages.
58: - @mentions: autocomplete, highlighted mention, server-validated atomic mention records, desktop notification (DM, 
group, room, thread reply) with browser-wide viewing suppression, feed highlight.
59: - Messaging metrics as redacted structured server logs via an authenticated, rate-limited application route.
60: - Phase 4 follow-ups: membership check inside `mark_conversation_read`; `get_unread_counts` not executable by 
`anon`; restore the open conversation after page reload.
61: 
62: ### Out of scope
63: - Loading the application while offline (service worker / offline app shell) (D2).
64: - Queuing attachments or voice notes (they keep the Phase 4 behavior).
65: - Editing a pending message (cancel and rewrite instead).
66: - Web Push / notifications with the browser or tab closed; cross-device notification suppression or dismissal.
67: - `@todos` / `@here`; self-mentions; a separate mention-only notification preference; scrolling to the message on 
notification click.
68: - Draft persistence; live sync of stars.
69: - Usage/engagement metrics; a metrics UI or external analytics service.
70: - Immediate revocation of typing signals for a tampered client of a removed member (accepted residual risk ≤ 
access-token lifetime, D3).
71: - Safari and mobile browsers; drawer layout below ~400 px.
72: - Other Phase 4 follow-ups: pending-upload cap, toaster covering the composer, pin no-op/English copy, orphan 
storage objects, "Favoritas" discoverability.
73: - Deploying the application (owner action).
74: 
75: ## 6. User journeys
76: 
77: - J1 Offline send: A loses internet and keeps writing; each message appears as pending; the banner says "Sem 
conexão — mensagens serão enviadas quando voltar". Internet returns; within 10 s every pending message is delivered in 
order and B sees each once.
78: - J2 Reload/close: A has pending messages and reloads while offline (the browser shows its normal offline error) 
or closes the tab. When the page loads again with internet, the pending messages are there and are sent.
79: - J3 Two tabs: A's pending message shows in both tabs; only one tab sends it; if that tab closes mid-send, the 
other finishes it once; cancelling a waiting message in either tab removes it from both.
80: - J4 Rejection: A's pending message targets a room A has since lost access to; it becomes a visible failure with 
the text kept and "Tentar de novo" / "Apagar"; later pending messages for that room wait; other conversations still 
deliver.
81: - J5 Sign-out: A clicks Sair with 2 unsent messages; a dialog warns that leaving deletes them; Cancelar leaves A 
exactly where A was; Sair mesmo signs A out.
82: - J6 Shared browser: A's session ends without Sair; C signs in on the same browser; C never sees A's messages and 
nothing is sent as C; when A signs in again, A's messages are sent as A.
83: - J7 Realtime outage: after 5 s the banner shows "Reconectando…"; after 30 s the open conversation and the list 
update every 15 s; when Realtime returns, polling stops and the banner disappears.
84: - J8 Typing: B types continuously in a group; A sees "Bruno está digitando…" the whole time; it disappears ≤ 5 s 
after B stops.
85: - J9 Two devices: A reads part of conversation X on the laptop; within 5 s the desktop's badge and read state for 
X match.
86: - J10 Mention: A types "@" in a room chat and picks Bruno; Bruno's tab is hidden; Bruno gets "Ana mencionou você 
em <sala>" with the full text; clicking opens the room; the message is highlighted for Bruno.
87: - J11 Reload restore: A reloads with the drawer open on conversation X; the drawer reopens on X.
88: 
89: ## 7. Functional requirements
90: 
91: | ID | Requirement | Priority | Acceptance signal |
92: |---|---|---|---|
93: | FR-001 | Every eligible send (text and/or reply, no attachments/voice; online or offline) is first written 
durably to the current user's outbox with its `clientMessageId`, conversation, content, reply target, and mention IDs, 
and only then dispatched and the composer cleared. The item shows in the feed as pending while not confirmed by the 
server. | Must | AC-001, AC-003, AC-040 |
94: | FR-002 | Pending items are sent automatically when connectivity returns (browser online, Realtime recovery, or 
periodic retry), per conversation in creation order, one in flight per conversation, always with their own 
`clientMessageId`. With ≤ 10 pending and quota available, all are delivered within 10 s of connectivity returning; 
with more, delivery continues within the rate limit. A confirmed item becomes the normal sent message and leaves the 
outbox. | Must | AC-002, AC-009, AC-038 |
95: | FR-003 | The outbox survives reload and tab close. Reloading while offline shows the browser's normal offline 
error; when the app loads again, the pending items are shown and sent. Items of the current user appear in every tab 
of the same browser. | Must | AC-004 |
96: | FR-004 | Cross-tab ownership: at most one tab holds the claim to dispatch a given item at a time (atomic claim); 
a claim is released when its tab closes or crashes, and another tab then resumes the item (reconciling first, FR-006); 
no tab dispatches an item while another live tab holds its claim; at most one create request per `clientMessageId` is 
in progress at any time across tabs. | Must | AC-005, AC-041 |
97: | FR-005 | Cancel: an item can be cancelled from any tab only if it is known not to be stored (never dispatched, 
or last attempt definitively rejected, or reconciled as not stored); the cancel is an atomic transition that wins or 
loses against a claim — a cancelled item is never dispatched afterwards, and an item already dispatched shows 
"enviando" without cancel. | Must | AC-006 |
98: | FR-006 | Unknown outcome: when a dispatched request ends without a response (network error, timeout, tab closed) 
or with a 5xx, the item is in unknown state. Before any cancel, expiry, or new dispatch, the app reconciles it with a 
read-only server lookup by `clientMessageId` (which never creates a message): if stored, the item resolves as 
delivered; if not stored, it returns to waiting. | Must | AC-038, AC-042 |
99: | FR-007 | A definitive rejection (BR-005) stops automatic attempts for that item; it shows as failed with its 
text and actions "Tentar de novo" and "Apagar"; later items of the same conversation wait until it is resolved; other 
conversations are unaffected. For `CLIENT_MESSAGE_ID_REUSED`, "Tentar de novo" sends the same content under a new 
`clientMessageId`. | Must | AC-007, AC-008, AC-043 |
100: | FR-008 | A rate-limit rejection is transient: the item stays pending and is retried automatically, preserving 
order and without duplicates. A create replay that returns an already-stored message does not consume the create 
quota. | Must | AC-009 |
101: | FR-009 | An item still not stored 24 h after the user pressed send is never sent automatically: once reconciled 
as not stored, it shows as failed "expirada" with "Tentar de novo" (manual send, same key) and "Apagar"; an unknown 
item found stored on reconciliation resolves as delivered, never as expired. | Must | AC-010, AC-042 |
102: | FR-010 | Attachments/voice are never queued. A file whose upload fails offline keeps the existing per-file 
error and retry (send stays disabled until uploads finish); a message whose uploads succeeded but whose create request 
fails keeps the Phase 4 composer failure and "Tentar de novo". | Must | AC-011 |
103: | FR-011 | Identity fencing: each item belongs to an application user (`users.id`). Before every dispatch the app 
checks that the authenticated user equals the item's owner; every dispatch carries the expected sender, and the create 
route rejects (without storing) a request whose authenticated user differs. When the authenticated user changes or 
signs out in any tab, all drains in all tabs stop; completions that arrive afterwards do not change another user's 
view. | Must | AC-012, AC-044 |
104: | FR-012 | Session end without explicit sign-out: an item is held (not failed, not deleted) only when the 
client's auth state reports the session ended (signed out, or refresh rejected); a 401 while the client still holds a 
valid session is treated as transient. Held items drain after the same user signs in again (subject to FR-009); 
another user on the browser never sees, sends, or deletes them. | Must | AC-012 |
105: | FR-013 | When the user clicks Sair at any in-app sign-out entry point with N > 0 unsent items, a dialog shows 
"Você tem N mensagens não enviadas. Sair apaga essas mensagens." with "Sair mesmo" and "Cancelar". Cancelar produces 
no logout request, success notice, or navigation. Sair mesmo stops drains in all tabs, deletes that user's outbox, and 
runs the existing sign-out (including the Presence logout sequence unchanged). With N = 0 sign-out is unchanged. | 
Must | AC-013 |
106: | FR-014 | Banner: "Sem conexão — mensagens serão enviadas quando voltar" as soon as the browser reports offline; 
"Reconectando…" when any required messaging Realtime stream (messages/receipts/reactions, conversation list) has been 
unhealthy for more than 5 s while the browser is online; it disappears by itself once all are healthy. | Must | AC-014 
|
107: | FR-015 | Every messaging Realtime stream (messages/receipts/reactions, conversation list, typing of the open 
conversation) has application-level health and recovery: after an error, timeout, or terminal closure it rejoins 
without user action using exponential backoff with a capped delay (in addition to the SDK's own retries), and on 
recovery the messages and conversation-list streams trigger a catch-up refetch of the open feed and the list; the 
typing stream rejoins. | Must | AC-015 |
108: | FR-016 | Polling fallback: while any required stream has been unhealthy for more than 30 s and the tab is 
visible, the app fetches the open conversation's new messages and the conversation list every 15 s; on becoming 
visible while still unhealthy it fetches immediately, then every 15 s; no polling while hidden; once all streams are 
healthy no new polling request starts. Results merged from polling and Realtime never duplicate or degrade messages. | 
Must | AC-016 |
109: | FR-017 | The typing indicator stays visible while the other person types continuously and disappears at most 5 
s after they stop, blur the composer, send, leave the conversation, or close the tab. | Must | AC-017 |
110: | FR-018 | The indicator names typists: 1 → "Ana está digitando…", 2 → "Ana e Bruno estão digitando…", 3+ → "N 
pessoas estão digitando…", in direct, group, and room conversations. | Must | AC-018 |
111: | FR-019 | Typing signals travel only on a private Realtime topic whose join and send are authorized for current 
conversation members; anon clients, authenticated non-members, and public-topic attempts never receive them; the app 
removes a member's client from the topic as soon as it learns of the removal. Payload: user identity and typing flag 
only. Residual risk accepted (D3): a tampered client of a removed member may receive typing signals until its access 
token expires. | Must | AC-019 |
112: | FR-020 | A user never sees their own typing indicator, including from their other tabs or devices. | Must | 
AC-020 |
113: | FR-021 | Within 5 s and without reload, focus change, or reconnect: when the user reads messages (fully or 
partly) on one tab/device, their other tabs/devices show the same unread counts and read state; the sender's receipt 
summary/details agree with the stored receipts on all of the sender's sessions. | Must | AC-021 |
114: | FR-022 | Within 5 s and without reload, a message the user sends from one device/tab appears on their other 
devices/tabs. | Must | AC-022 |
115: | FR-023 | In the composer, typing "@" opens a list of the conversation's current members except the user, 
filtered as they type, operable by mouse and keyboard (arrows, Enter/Tab, Esc); choosing inserts a highlighted 
mention. In a room the list contains only members of the room conversation. | Must | AC-023 |
116: | FR-024 | The server stores mentions in the same transaction as the message, as application user IDs of 
conversation members at send time other than the sender; other IDs and "@name" text create no mention. Mentions are 
part of the idempotent composition (same key with different mentions → `CLIENT_MESSAGE_ID_REUSED`). Clients cannot 
write mention records directly; only conversation members can read them. | Must | AC-024 |
117: | FR-025 | With the Phase 4 desktop-notification toggle on and permission granted, a mentioned user receives one 
desktop notification for a message that mentions them (direct, group, room, thread reply), decided from authoritative 
mention data, titled "<Autor> mencionou você em <conversa>" with the full text; clicking opens the conversation. 
Suppression is browser-wide: no tab notifies if any of the user's tabs in that browser is visible and viewing that 
conversation, regardless of which tab receives the event first. | Must | AC-025, AC-026 |
118: | FR-026 | Mentions render distinctly for everyone; a message that mentions the viewing user is highlighted for 
that user with a non-color cue. | Must | AC-027 |
119: | FR-027 | The client records metric events — send latency (press → server confirmed), outbox wait time, send 
outcomes by reason, Realtime stream drops/recoveries and outage duration, polling activations and duration — in a 
bounded buffer (oldest dropped when full) and sends them in batches, fire-and-forget, to an authenticated, per-user 
rate-limited application route. The route accepts only closed event/reason enums and bounded numeric fields, drops 
anything else, adds a server-derived app version, and writes structured log lines under BR-013. | Must | AC-028 |
120: | FR-028 | Metrics never block, slow, or break messaging: messaging code never awaits metric delivery; an 
erroring or hanging metrics route changes no messaging behavior or timing and shows no error. | Must | AC-029 |
121: | FR-029 | `mark_conversation_read` refuses to mark a conversation for a user who is not a member of it. | Must | 
AC-030 |
122: | FR-030 | `get_unread_counts` is not executable by the `anon` role; authenticated use keeps working. | Must | 
AC-031 |
123: | FR-031 | After a page reload, if the drawer was open, it reopens on the same conversation for the same user on 
the same browser; if that conversation is no longer accessible, the conversation list opens without an error; a closed 
drawer stays closed. | Must | AC-032 |
124: | FR-032 | Existing messaging behavior (Phase 4 flows: receipts, attachments, voice notes, stars, DM/group 
notifications and their once-across-tabs claim, failed-send retry for attachment messages, reconnect catch-up) does 
not regress. | Must | AC-033 |
125: 
126: ## 8. Business rules
127: 
128: | ID | Rule | Rationale |
129: |---|---|---|
130: | BR-001 | The outbox belongs to one application user (`users.id`) on one browser; it is never shown, dispatched, 
or deleted while another user is authenticated, and the server never stores an item under a sender other than its 
owner. | R1-q3 A, R1-q16 A, R2-q5 A; review r2 #6 |
131: | BR-002 | Only text messages and replies without attachments/voice are queued. | R1-q4 A |
132: | BR-003 | Per conversation, items are sent in creation order, one in flight at a time; a failed (definitive or 
expired) item blocks later items of that conversation until the user retries or deletes it. Conversations are 
independent. | R1-q5 A, R1-q2 E |
133: | BR-004 | Each item gets a stable `clientMessageId` when the user presses send; every attempt reuses it (except 
the `CLIENT_MESSAGE_ID_REUSED` retry, FR-007); the server stores at most one message per (sender, conversation, 
`clientMessageId`). | R1-q2 B, R1-q16 B |
134: | BR-005 | Outcome classes: transient = browser offline before dispatch, HTTP 429, HTTP 401 while the client 
session is valid → retry automatically; unknown = network error/timeout/no response after dispatch, HTTP 5xx → 
reconcile (FR-006); session-ended = client auth reports signed out / refresh rejected → hold (FR-012); definitive = 
403 `NOT_PARTICIPANT`, 404, 400, 413 `CONTENT_TOO_LARGE`, 422, 409 `CLIENT_MESSAGE_ID_REUSED`, sender mismatch → 
failed, no automatic retry. | R1-q5 C, R2-q4 A, R2-q5 A; review r2 #2, #11 |
135: | BR-006 | Expiry: an item not stored 24 h after the user pressed send becomes "expirada" and is never sent 
automatically; a manual "Tentar de novo" sends it with the same key. | R1-q5 D, R2-q3 B |
136: | BR-007 | Cancel only for items known not to be stored and not claimed; a cancelled item is removed in all tabs 
and never dispatched. | R1-q5 B, R2-q7 A; review r2 #2, #5 |
137: | BR-008 | An explicit user sign-out with unsent items requires confirmation; confirming deletes that user's 
outbox; a sign-out not initiated by the user keeps it. Cancelling has no side effect. | R2-q5 A, R2-q6 A |
138: | BR-009 | Typing: only current conversation members join and send on a conversation's typing topic; a user's own 
typing (any session) is never shown to them; an indicator expires ≤ 5 s after the last signal; residual exposure to a 
tampered removed-member client is bounded by access-token lifetime (D3). | R1-q8 A, D, E; R1-q16 E; D3 |
139: | BR-010 | Mentions: only current members of the conversation at send time, other than the sender, can be 
mentioned (rooms: members of the room conversation); no `@todos`/`@here`. | R1-q10 A, E; R2-q10 A; D4 |
140: | BR-011 | Desktop-notification eligibility (extends Phase 4): toggle on and permission granted; never for the 
user's own message; never while any tab of the user in that browser is visible and viewing that conversation; 
DM/group: every new message; room: only messages that mention the user; at most one notification per message per 
browser. | R1-q10 B, R1-q16 C, R2-q8 A, R2-q9 A–C; review r2 #9 |
141: | BR-012 | Polling runs only while a required Realtime stream has been unhealthy for more than 30 s and the tab 
is visible, every 15 s (immediately on becoming visible), and no new poll starts once all streams are healthy. | R1-q7 
A, R1-q16 D, R2-q12 C |
142: | BR-013 | Metric log lines contain only closed-enum event type and outcome/reason, bounded durations/counts, 
conversation type, timestamp, and server-derived app version — never message text, attachment names, conversation 
names, user names or emails, user/conversation identifiers, or any client-supplied free text. | R1-q12 B, R2-q2, 
confirmation point 9; review r2 #14 |
143: | BR-014 | Database rollout: migrations stay local during the phase (written + applied locally with recorded 
rollback SQL); at phase end they are applied to `vhabpcoyypobgasacsko` only with explicit owner authorization, 
followed by catalog readback and a runtime smoke check; the application is deployed by the owner after the migrations; 
rollback is app first, then migrations. | R1-q13 A |
144: | BR-015 | Tests: only integration (real local Supabase/Realtime/API routes) and E2E (real UI, Playwright); no 
new unit tests; a touched unit test is replaced by integration/E2E coverage and deleted, recorded in TRACK. Each new 
E2E spec counts only after 3 consecutive passing runs. | AGENTS.md test policy, R2-q13 A |
145: | BR-016 | Conversation restore is per browser and per user; a different user never inherits it. | R2-q11 A, B, D 
|
