166: ## 10. Constraints and assumptions
167: 
168: ### Constraints
169: - Next.js App Router, TypeScript strict, Supabase, TanStack Query; reuse existing hooks, repositories, routes, 
and query keys rather than parallel implementations.
170: - Identity: RLS compares `users.supabase_uid` with `auth.uid()::text`; foreign keys use `users.id`; messages link 
to rooms through `conversations.room_id`.
171: - Browsers: Chrome and Edge desktop (automated E2E); Firefox desktop (verified). Safari and mobile not required.
172: - No service worker; background notifications are page-level only.
173: - No message text, names, or identifiers in metrics (BR-013); no external analytics service.
174: - The sign-out confirmation must not change the Presence logout sequence (presence-safety rules apply to that 
touch point).
175: - Online database changes only at phase end with explicit authorization (BR-014); the hosted app currently runs 
the pre-Phase-4 build until the owner deploys.
176: - Repository test policy (BR-015); type-check, lint, and build must pass.
177: 
178: ### Assumptions
179: - None beyond the user-confirmed answers and owner decisions recorded in `state.md`.
180: 
181: ## 11. Edge cases and failure modes
182: 
183: | Case | Expected behavior |
184: |---|---|
185: | Tab closed right after pressing send, before server confirmation | Item is already in the outbox; on next load 
it is reconciled and delivered once (AC-040). |
186: | Request stored but response lost | Item becomes unknown; lookup finds it stored; resolves as delivered; never 
offered for cancel or expired (AC-038, AC-042). |
187: | Two tabs come online together | One claim per item; at most one create request per key at a time; each stored 
and shown once (AC-005). |
188: | Owning tab closes or hangs mid-request | Closed: another tab reconciles and finishes once. Hung but alive: no 
other tab dispatches that item (AC-041). |
189: | Cancel races a claim | Exactly one wins; a cancelled item is never dispatched; a dispatched item cannot be 
cancelled (AC-006). |
190: | Lost access before reconnect | First item fails definitively with text; later items of that conversation wait; 
other conversations deliver (AC-007). |
191: | Access regained after failure | "Tentar de novo" delivers it, then the waiting items in order (AC-008). |
192: | `CLIENT_MESSAGE_ID_REUSED` | Failed; "Tentar de novo" resends the content under a new key, once (AC-043). |
193: | Server rate limit during drain | Items stay pending, retried automatically, order and uniqueness preserved; 
replays do not consume quota (AC-009). |
194: | Laptop asleep > 24 h | Not-stored items become "expirada"; stored ones resolve as delivered (AC-010, AC-042). |
195: | Offline attachment / voice | Upload failure: per-file error + retry; create failure after upload: Phase 4 
composer failure; nothing in the outbox (AC-011). |
196: | IndexedDB unavailable | Eligible send keeps the Phase 4 composer failure + "Tentar de novo" on failure — never 
silently dropped (AC-039). |
197: | Account switched while a drain is paused | Drain stops; no dispatch as the new user; server rejects any request 
whose sender differs from the item owner (AC-044). |
198: | Session ends without Sair; other user signs in | Other user never sees/sends A's items; A's items drain after A 
signs in (AC-012). |
199: | Temporary Auth failure (401 with valid client session) | Treated as transient; retried; not held or deleted 
(AC-012). |
200: | Sair with unsent items | Dialog; Cancelar has no side effects at any entry point; Sair mesmo stops drains 
everywhere and deletes the outbox (AC-013). |
201: | Realtime drop < 5 s | No "Reconectando…" banner (AC-014). |
202: | Only the conversation-list stream fails | Detected and recovered independently; banner/polling follow its 
health (AC-015, AC-016). |
203: | Polling and Realtime deliver the same message | Shown once, not degraded (AC-016). |
204: | Realtime down > 30 s, tab hidden | No polling; on visible, fetch immediately then every 15 s (AC-016). |
205: | Typist closes the tab mid-typing | Indicator disappears ≤ 5 s (AC-017). |
206: | Anon, outsider, public-topic, or removed-member typing access | Refused / no events; removed member's app 
leaves at once; tampered client bounded by D3 (AC-019). |
207: | Forged mention IDs or changed mentions on replay | No mention for non-members/self; changed mentions under one 
key → 409 (AC-024). |
208: | Hidden tab receives a mention event first while another tab views that conversation | No notification (AC-026). 
|
209: | Malicious or hanging metrics traffic | Unknown fields/free text dropped or rejected; route rate-limited; 
messaging unaffected (AC-028, AC-029). |
210: | Restored conversation no longer accessible | Conversation list opens without error (AC-032). |
211: 
212: ## 12. Security, privacy, compliance, and abuse considerations
213: - Outbox content lives only in the user's browser, scoped to the application user ID, fenced against account 
switches on the client and on the server (expected-sender check), and deleted on confirmed explicit sign-out.
214: - The read-only lookup returns only the caller's own message for that key and never creates.
215: - Typing topics are private and members-only for join and send (Realtime RLS with `users.supabase_uid = 
auth.uid()::text` → `users.id`); residual exposure to tampered removed-member clients accepted (D3).
216: - Mentions are validated and stored server-side in the message transaction; clients cannot write them directly; 
only members can read them.
217: - Notifications never leak content of conversations the user is not a member of.
218: - Metrics: closed enums, bounded numbers, server-derived version, per-user rate limit, no free text (BR-013); 
unauthenticated requests are rejected and produce no metric log line.
219: - `mark_conversation_read` membership check and `anon` revoke on `get_unread_counts` close two Phase 4 findings.
220: - No service-role key in client code; service-role use never replaces application authorization checks.
221: - Every migration, RLS/Realtime policy, repository, and API route change passes the Supabase/RLS review gate.
222: 
223: ## 13. Accessibility, localization, and usability considerations
224: - UI copy in Brazilian Portuguese. The banner text, sign-out dialog text, typing phrases, and mention 
notification title in this SPEC are required; item-state labels ("pendente", "enviando", "expirada") and actions 
("Cancelar", "Tentar de novo", "Apagar") are proposed, and equivalent Portuguese wording with the same meaning is 
accepted (D4).
225: - Connection banner uses a polite live region; item states have text labels, not only icons.
226: - Item actions and the sign-out dialog are keyboard-operable with visible focus; the dialog traps focus and Esc 
means Cancelar.
227: - The mention list follows the combobox/listbox pattern and is announced to screen readers.
228: - The mention highlight does not rely on color alone.
229: 
230: ## 14. Acceptance criteria
231: 
232: - AC-001 (FR-001, BR-002): Given A's browser context is offline, when A sends a text message, then A's feed shows 
it as pending with a text label, the composer is empty, the outbox holds it, and B does not see it.
233: - AC-002 (FR-002, BR-003, BR-004): Given A queued 3 messages offline in conversation X with quota available, when 
connectivity returns, then within 10 s and without any click B sees exactly those 3 messages, once each, in the 
original order, and A's items become normal sent messages.
234: - AC-003 (FR-001, BR-005): Given the browser reports online but A's create request fails with a network error 
after dispatch, then the item becomes unknown, is reconciled, and is delivered exactly once without user action once 
requests succeed.
235: - AC-004 (FR-003): Given A has a pending item, when A reloads while offline, then the browser's offline error is 
shown; when the network returns and the app loads again, the item is shown as pending and is delivered exactly once. 
Given A closes the tab with a pending item, then reopening the app later delivers it once.
236: - AC-005 (FR-004, BR-004; must-not R1-q16 B): Given A has two tabs and queued 2 messages offline in tab 1, then 
both appear in tab 2; when both tabs come back online simultaneously, then the network log of both tabs shows at most 
one create request in progress per `clientMessageId` at any time, B receives each message exactly once, and the 
database holds one row per key.
237: - AC-006 (FR-005, BR-007): Given a waiting item never dispatched, when A cancels it in tab 2 at the moment tab 1 
tries to claim it, then either the cancel wins (the item disappears from both tabs and no create request for it is 
ever sent) or the claim wins (no cancel action is offered and the item is delivered once); given a dispatched item, it 
shows "enviando" and offers no cancel.
238: - AC-007 (FR-007, BR-003, BR-005): Given A queued m1 and m2 in room conversation X and m3 in DM Y, and A lost 
access to X before reconnecting, when connectivity returns, then m1 shows failed with its text and "Tentar de novo" / 
"Apagar", m2 stays waiting with no create request sent, and m3 is delivered.
239: - AC-008 (FR-007, BR-003): Given AC-007's state, when A regains access to X and clicks "Tentar de novo" on m1, 
then m1 and then m2 are delivered in order; given instead A clicks "Apagar" on m1, then m1 is never delivered and m2 
is attempted next.
240: - AC-009 (FR-002, FR-008, BR-005): Given A's create quota is exhausted when A's 12 queued items start draining, 
then rejected items stay pending and retry automatically, and B finally sees all 12 in order, once each; a replay of 
an already-stored message returns it without reducing the remaining quota (integration test on the real route).
241: - AC-010 (FR-009, BR-006): Given a never-dispatched item whose send time is more than 24 h ago, when connectivity 
returns, then no create request is sent for it and it shows "expirada" with "Tentar de novo" / "Apagar"; "Tentar de 
novo" delivers it once.
242: - AC-011 (FR-010, BR-002): Given A is offline, when A adds a file, then its upload shows the existing per-file 
error and retry, Send stays disabled, and nothing enters the outbox; given A's uploads succeeded and then the network 
drops, when A sends, then the message stays in the composer with the Phase 4 failure and "Tentar de novo", and nothing 
enters the outbox.
243: - AC-012 (FR-011, FR-012, BR-001, BR-005; must-not R1-q16 A): Given A has unsent items and A's browser session 
ends without Sair (auth cookies/storage cleared or refresh token revoked so the client reports signed out), when C 
signs in on the same browser, then C sees none of A's items and no create request is sent while C is authenticated; 
when A signs in again, A's items (< 24 h) are delivered as A. Given a 401 while A's client session is valid, then the 
item stays pending and is retried.
244: - AC-013 (FR-013, BR-008): Given A has 2 unsent items, when A clicks Sair from each in-app entry point, then the 
dialog shows "Você tem 2 mensagens não enviadas. Sair apaga essas mensagens."; Cancelar sends no logout request, shows 
no notice, and does not navigate; Sair mesmo with a drain in flight in another tab stops all drains, deletes the 
outbox, and signs out with the Presence logout sequence as before; after A signs in again the outbox is empty. Given 0 
unsent items, Sair signs out without a dialog.
245: - AC-014 (FR-014): Given the drawer is open, when the browser goes offline, then "Sem conexão — mensagens serão 
enviadas quando voltar" appears immediately; when only Realtime drops for 3 s, no banner appears; when a required 
stream stays unhealthy for more than 5 s, "Reconectando…" appears; when all recover, the banner disappears without 
user action.
246: - AC-015 (FR-015): Given each required stream is dropped independently (messages stream only; conversation-list 
stream only, including a terminal closure; typing stream only), when Realtime becomes available again, then that 
stream recovers without user action; B's messages sent during the outage appear once; unread counts are correct; 
typing works again; recorded recovery events show increasing, capped retry delays.
247: - AC-016 (FR-016, BR-012; must-not R1-q16 D): Given Realtime is blocked while HTTP works and A's tab is visible, 
when B sends a message at the outage start, then A sees it within 45 s; a message B sends after polling started 
appears within 15 s plus request time; the list's unread counts update. When A's tab is hidden, no polling requests 
are made; when it becomes visible, a fetch happens immediately. When Realtime recovers while a poll is in flight, the 
message delivered by both appears once, and no new polling request starts during the next 60 s.
248: - AC-017 (FR-017; must-not R1-q16 E): Given B types continuously for 15 s, then A sees the typing indicator at 
every 1 s sample; when B stops, it disappears within 5 s; when B closes the tab mid-typing, it disappears within 5 s.
249: - AC-018 (FR-018): Given a group or room with A, B, C, D, when B types, A sees "<B> está digitando…"; B and C → 
"<B> e <C> estão digitando…"; B, C, D → "3 pessoas estão digitando…"; a direct conversation shows the single-typist 
phrase.
250: - AC-019 (FR-019, BR-009): With real local Realtime: an anon client, an authenticated non-member, and a client on 
the public topic name receive no typing events of conversation X and cannot send typing events members receive; member 
A receives B's typing; when member E is removed from X, E's app leaves the topic and shows/receives no further typing 
for X (integration + E2E).
251: - AC-020 (FR-020, BR-009): Given A has two tabs and a second device on X, when A types in tab 1, then neither tab 
2 nor the second device shows A as typing.
252: - AC-021 (FR-021): Given A on two devices with 6 unread in X, when A reads only the first 3 on device 1, then 
within 5 s device 2 shows 3 unread for X; after reading the rest, 0. Given A is the sender of messages read by B, then 
A's receipt summary/details on both of A's sessions match the stored receipts within 5 s.
253: - AC-022 (FR-022): Given A on two devices, when A sends on device 1, then it appears on device 2 within 5 s 
without reload.
254: - AC-023 (FR-023, BR-010): Given A types "@" in a room conversation, then the list shows only room-conversation 
members other than A, filters as A types, and supports arrows/Enter/Tab/Esc and mouse; choosing "Bruno" inserts a 
highlighted mention; a workspace user who never joined the room chat is not listed.
255: - AC-024 (FR-024, BR-010; must-not R1-q16 F): On the real route and local DB: a message mentioning a non-member 
ID and the sender's own ID stores no mention for either and notifies neither; a replay under the same key with 
different mention IDs returns `CLIENT_MESSAGE_ID_REUSED` and changes nothing; a forced failure of mention storage 
stores neither the message nor any mention; anon and non-members cannot read mention records; no client can 
insert/update/delete them directly.
256: - AC-025 (FR-025, BR-011): Given B has notifications enabled and permission granted, B's tab hidden or viewing 
another conversation, when A mentions B in a room, then B receives exactly one notification "<A> mencionou você em 
<sala>" with the full text; clicking opens that room in the drawer; a room message without a mention of B produces 
none.
257: - AC-026 (FR-025, BR-011; must-not R1-q16 C): Given B has notifications enabled, then a DM mention yields exactly 
one notification; a thread-reply mention yields one; with B's two tabs where the hidden tab receives the event first 
and the visible tab is viewing that conversation, none; A's own message yields none for A; with the toggle off, none; 
a mention across B's two hidden tabs yields exactly one.
258: - AC-027 (FR-026): Given A's message mentions B, then all members see the mention styled distinctly, and only B 
sees the whole message highlighted with a non-color cue.
259: - AC-028 (FR-027, BR-013): Given a send, a stream recovery, and a polling episode occur, then the metrics route 
writes structured lines with enum event type/reason, durations, and server-derived version; a scan finds no message 
text, names, emails, or user/conversation IDs; a payload with unknown fields, free-text strings, out-of-range numbers, 
or a forged version produces no such values in the logs; requests beyond the per-user rate limit are rejected; an 
unauthenticated request is rejected and produces no metric log line.
260: - AC-029 (FR-028): Given the metrics route errors, and separately hangs without responding, when A sends, goes 
offline, and reconnects, then AC-001/AC-002 behavior and timing hold and no error is shown.
261: - AC-030 (FR-029): Given user E is not a member of X, when `mark_conversation_read` is invoked for E and X 
through every role that can execute it, then the call is refused and E's read state for X is unchanged (integration, 
local DB).
262: - AC-031 (FR-030): Given the `anon` role, when it calls `get_unread_counts`, then permission is denied; an 
authenticated member still gets correct counts (integration, local DB).
263: - AC-032 (FR-031, BR-016): Given the drawer open on X, when A reloads, then it reopens on X; given A lost access 
to X, then the list opens with no error; given C signs in on that browser, then C does not get A's conversation; given 
the drawer was closed, it stays closed.
264: - AC-033 (FR-032): The existing Phase 4 messaging E2E regression suite and DB integration suite pass after each 
slice; `npm run type-check`, `npm run lint`, and `npm run build` pass.
265: - AC-034 (BR-014): Every migration of the phase is applied locally with recorded rollback SQL; at phase end, 
after explicit owner authorization, it is applied to `vhabpcoyypobgasacsko`, the catalog is read back from that 
target, and a runtime smoke check passes; the hand-off states the app is ready to deploy after the migrations.
266: - AC-035 (§12): The Supabase/RLS review gate passes for every migration, RLS/Realtime policy, repository, and API 
route change.
267: - AC-036 (BR-015): Each new E2E spec passed 3 consecutive runs in Chrome and in Edge; the key flows (outbox, 
banner, typing, mentions) were verified in Firefox and recorded; no unit test was added; every touched unit test was 
replaced and deleted, recorded in TRACK.
268: - AC-037 (FR-014, §13): The banner is announced via a polite live region, item states have text labels, and the 
sign-out dialog and mention list are fully keyboard-operable.
269: - AC-038 (FR-002, FR-006, BR-004): Given A's create request is stored but its response is dropped, then the item 
becomes unknown, the lookup resolves it as delivered without a second create, and B sees it once.
270: - AC-039 (FR-001, R1-q2 A): Given IndexedDB is unavailable, when A's eligible send fails, then it stays in the 
composer with the Phase 4 failure and "Tentar de novo" — not lost.
271: - AC-040 (FR-001): Given A is online and the create request is held, when A closes the tab before the server 
confirms, then on reopening the app the item is reconciled and B sees the message exactly once.
272: - AC-041 (FR-004): Given tab 1 holds the claim and its create request is held, then tab 2 sends no create request 
for that item while tab 1 is alive; when tab 1 is closed, tab 2 reconciles and the message is stored once.
273: - AC-042 (FR-006, FR-009): Given an unknown item whose request was actually stored, when A tries to cancel it or 
its 24 h passes, then no cancel is offered before reconciliation, the lookup resolves it as delivered, and it never 
shows "expirada".
274: - AC-043 (FR-007): Given an item rejected with `CLIENT_MESSAGE_ID_REUSED`, when A clicks "Tentar de novo", then 
its content is stored once under a new key and the conflicting stored message is unchanged.
275: - AC-044 (FR-011, BR-001): Given A's drain is paused mid-item and the browser's session switches to C (a member 
of the same conversation), when the drain would resume, then no create request is sent as C; a crafted create request 
carrying A as expected sender with C's session is rejected and stores nothing.
276: 
277: ## 15. Test strategy
278: 
279: - Test levels:
280:   - Integration (local Supabase Postgres/Realtime and real API routes): sender fencing and quota-free replay on 
create (AC-009, AC-044), lookup route (AC-038, AC-042 server side), mention atomicity/validation/RLS (AC-024), private 
typing-topic authorization incl. anon/outsider/public topic (AC-019), `mark_conversation_read` membership (AC-030), 
`get_unread_counts` anon revoke (AC-031), metrics route auth, enum validation, rate limit, redaction (AC-028).
281:   - E2E (Playwright, real UI; two or more real accounts; two tabs of one account; a second device as a separate 
browser context): every other AC. Outages: browser-context offline mode and blocking only the Realtime WebSocket or a 
single stream, in both directions; lost responses via request pass-through then response drop; held requests via 
delayed pass-through to the real server. These shape the network only; the app, routes, and Supabase stay real. Expiry 
may use a test-controlled clock without changing the product rule.
282:   - Unit: none (BR-015).
283: - Tests trace to acceptance criteria by AC ID; written from the ACs before or with the implementation.
284: - Test data: accounts A, B, C, D, E in one company; a DM, a group, and a room conversation; a room the test can 
remove A/E from and re-add; a way to exhaust A's create quota locally.
285: - Environments: local Supabase for integration/E2E; Chrome and Edge for automated E2E; Firefox verification of 
key flows recorded; online target only for phase-end readback and smoke (BR-014).
286: - Reliability: each new E2E spec counts only after 3 consecutive passing runs (BR-015).
287: - Mandatory failure-path tests: every AC-003..AC-013 and AC-038..AC-044 scenario, short vs long Realtime drop, 
independent stream failures, hidden-tab polling, polling/Realtime overlap, typing tab close, typing access by 
anon/outsider/removed member, forged/changed mentions, visible+hidden tab notification race, malicious and hanging 
metrics traffic, restore with lost access.
288: 
