1: -- Phase 4 T23 / FR-024, AC-029, AC-035
2: -- (spec-interview/phase-4-messaging-timeline): retrying a send never creates a
3: -- duplicate message.
4: --
5: -- The drawer composer generates one key (uuid) per composition and resends it
6: -- on every "Tentar de novo" of that composition. POST /api/messages/create
7: -- stores it in messages.client_message_id; a create repeating a key already
8: -- stored for the same sender and conversation returns the existing message
9: -- instead of inserting another. When a committed create loses its response
10: -- and the user retries, the server therefore keeps exactly one row.
11: --
12: -- Scope of uniqueness: (sender_id, conversation_id, client_message_id). The
13: -- sender is always the authenticated caller resolved on the server
14: -- (users.supabase_uid = auth.uid() -> users.id), so one user's key can never
15: -- match or block another user's message, and a key reused in another
16: -- conversation is a different message.
17: --
18: -- Concurrency: two simultaneous creates with the same key race on this unique
19: -- index; the loser gets unique_violation (23505), which the repository turns
20: -- into a read of the winning row (no 500, one row).
21: --
22: -- Backward compatible with the published app (BR-013):
23: --   * the column is nullable with no default; the published app sends no key,
24: --     its rows keep NULL and are outside the partial index, so legacy creates
25: --     behave exactly as before (any number of identical messages allowed);
26: --   * no existing column, policy, grant, trigger, or function changes; RLS on
27: --     messages already governs the new column like every other column;
28: --   * Realtime INSERT payloads gain one nullable field (ignored by the
29: --     published client mappers).
30: --
31: -- Locking/size: ADD COLUMN without a default is a catalog-only change but
32: -- takes a brief ACCESS EXCLUSIVE lock on messages; CREATE INDEX then holds a
33: -- SHARE lock (blocks writes) for one scan of the table. The partial index only
34: -- holds rows with a key (none at creation). lock_timeout makes the migration
35: -- fail fast instead of queueing message traffic behind a long transaction;
36: -- re-run it when that happens. Not CONCURRENTLY because migrations run inside
37: -- a transaction.
38: --
39: -- Transactional and re-runnable (if not exists).
40: --
41: -- Rollback (drops the key and the dedupe). Order: first roll the app back to
42: -- a build whose create route does not read/write client_message_id (the T23
43: -- route fails creates that carry a key once the column is gone; the published
44: -- app sends no key and is unaffected), then run:
45: --   begin;
46: --   drop index if exists public.messages_sender_conversation_client_message_id_key;
47: --   alter table public.messages drop column if exists client_message_id;
48: --   delete from supabase_migrations.schema_migrations where version = '20261005000414';
49: --   commit;
50: 
51: begin;
52: 
53: set local lock_timeout = '5s';
54: 
55: alter table public.messages
56:   add column if not exists client_message_id uuid;
57: 
58: comment on column public.messages.client_message_id is
59:   'Client-generated idempotency key of the composition that created this message (Phase 4 T23). NULL for messages 
created without a key. Unique per (sender_id, conversation_id).';
60: 
61: create unique index if not exists messages_sender_conversation_client_message_id_key
62:   on public.messages using btree (sender_id, conversation_id, client_message_id)
63:   where client_message_id is not null;
64: 
65: commit;

