9:The Virtual Office messaging drawer (`MessagingDrawer` → `MessageFeed` → `MessageComposer` / `MessageItem`, state in 
`MessagingContext`, data in `useMessages` / `useConversations`, Realtime in `useMessageSubscription` / 
`useConversationRealtime`, typing in `useConversationPresence`) was completed for timeline features in Phase 4. Phase 
4 also delivered a failed send that keeps text/reply/files with a single-message retry (`clientMessageId` 
idempotency), a Realtime catch-up after reconnect, and opt-in desktop notifications for DM/group messages. This phase 
adds: a persistent per-user offline outbox for text messages; a connection banner; automatic reconnection of every 
messaging Realtime channel with polling fallback; a reliable, named, members-only typing indicator; live cross-device 
unread sync; @mentions with desktop notifications (including rooms); messaging health metrics as redacted server logs; 
and two Phase 4 database follow-ups plus restoring the open conversation after reload.
15:## 3. Goals and success metrics
24:- G7: Messaging health (latency, delivery, connection) is observable in technical logs without exposing content.
27:### Success metrics
42:- The owner/operator, who reads the messaging metric logs.
50:- Persistent offline outbox (IndexedDB, per application user, per browser) for text messages and replies; pending 
state in the feed; cancel; ordered automatic drain; rejection, rate-limit, and 24 h expiry handling; session-expiry 
and sign-out rules.
57:- Messaging metrics as redacted structured server logs via an authenticated application route.
64:- `@todos` / `@here`; a separate mention-only notification preference; scrolling to the message on notification 
click.
66:- Usage/engagement metrics (messages per day, active conversations); a metrics UI or external analytics service.
88:| FR-001 | When A sends a message with text and/or a reply target and no attachments, and the browser is offline or 
the create request fails transiently (network error, timeout, 5xx, rate limit), the message is stored in A's 
persistent outbox and shown in the feed as pending (clock icon + "pendente" text) instead of the Phase 4 composer 
failure; the composer clears. | Must | AC-001, AC-003 |
90:| FR-003 | The outbox survives reload and closing the tab; pending messages of the current user appear in every tab 
of the same browser; at most one tab sends a given pending message at a time. | Must | AC-004, AC-005 |
93:| FR-006 | A rate-limit rejection is transient: the message stays pending and is retried automatically (honoring a 
server retry delay when provided), preserving order and without duplicates. | Must | AC-009 |
96:| FR-009 | The outbox is scoped to the application user (`users.id`) on that browser. If the session ends without 
an explicit sign-out (expiry, revocation), the outbox is kept and drains only after the same user signs in again 
(subject to FR-007); another user signing in on that browser never sees, sends, or deletes it. | Must | AC-012 |
97:| FR-010 | When the user clicks Sair (any in-app sign-out entry point) with N > 0 pending/failed outbox messages, a 
dialog shows "Você tem N mensagens não enviadas. Sair apaga essas mensagens." with "Sair mesmo" (deletes that user's 
outbox, then signs out) and "Cancelar" (keeps session and outbox). With N = 0 sign-out is unchanged. | Must | AC-013 |
111:| FR-024 | The client records metric events — send latency (press → server confirmed), time spent in the outbox, 
send outcomes by reason (success, no network, lost access, invalid, expired, rate limited), Realtime 
drops/reconnections and outage duration, polling activations and duration — and sends them in batches to an 
authenticated application route that writes structured log lines under BR-013. Unauthenticated requests are rejected 
and not logged. | Must | AC-028 |
112:| FR-025 | Metric collection and delivery never block, slow, or break messaging: if the metrics route fails, 
messaging behaves normally and no error is shown. | Must | AC-029 |
122:| BR-001 | The outbox belongs to one application user (`users.id`) on one browser. It is never read, sent, or 
deleted while another user is signed in. | R1-q3 A, R1-q16 A, R2-q5 A |
124:| BR-003 | Per conversation, outbox messages are sent in creation order, one in flight at a time; a failed 
(definitive or expired) message blocks later messages of that conversation until the user retries or deletes it. 
Conversations are independent. | R1-q5 A, R1-q2 E |
125:| BR-004 | Each outbox message gets a stable `clientMessageId` when the user presses send; every attempt 
(automatic, manual, any tab) reuses it; the server stores at most one message per (sender, conversation, 
`clientMessageId`). | R1-q2 B, R1-q16 B |
126:| BR-005 | Send outcome classes: transient = browser offline, network error, timeout, HTTP 5xx, rate limit (429) → 
stay pending and retry automatically; session = 401 → hold until the same user is authenticated again; definitive = no 
access / conversation not found (403 `NOT_PARTICIPANT`, 404), invalid content (400, 413 `CONTENT_TOO_LARGE`, 422), 
`CLIENT_MESSAGE_ID_REUSED` (409) → failed, no automatic retry. | R1-q5 C, R2-q4 A, R2-q5 A |
128:| BR-007 | Cancel is available only while a message waits (not in flight). A cancelled message is removed from the 
outbox in all tabs and never sent. | R1-q5 B, R2-q7 A |
129:| BR-008 | An explicit user sign-out with outbox messages requires confirmation; confirming deletes that user's 
outbox. A sign-out not initiated by the user (expiry, revocation) keeps it. | R2-q5 A, R2-q6 A |
144:- Server responses from `POST /api/messages/create` (status, `clientMessageId` replay, rate-limit retry delay when 
present).
147:- Browser IndexedDB outbox, keyed by application user ID: `clientMessageId`, conversation ID, text, reply target, 
mention user IDs, created-at, attempt state, failure reason. Deleted per BR-007/BR-008; never shared across users.
151:- Structured metric log lines (BR-013) from an authenticated application route.
157:- Hosting logs (Vercel) and/or Supabase logs as the metrics sink — no new external analytics dependency.
166:- No message text or names in metrics (BR-013); no new external analytics service.
179:| Two tabs come online at the same time with the same outbox | Only one tab sends each message; each is stored and 
shown once (AC-005). |
183:| Server rate limit during drain | Messages stay pending and retry automatically; order and uniqueness preserved 
(AC-009). |
187:| User clicks Sair with pending messages | Confirmation dialog; Cancelar keeps everything; Sair mesmo deletes the 
outbox and signs out (AC-013). |
205:- Metric logs follow BR-013 (no content, names, or identifiers); the metrics route requires authentication and 
bounds payload size and batch length to avoid log abuse.
227:- AC-009 (FR-006, BR-005): Given A's drain hits the server's create rate limit, then the rejected messages stay 
pending and are retried automatically, and B finally sees all of them in order, once each.
229:- AC-011 (FR-008, BR-002): Given A is offline, when A sends a message with a file or a voice note, then it stays 
in the composer with the Phase 4 failure message and "Tentar de novo", and nothing is added to the outbox.
231:- AC-013 (FR-010, BR-008): Given A has 2 outbox messages, when A clicks Sair, then the dialog shows "Você tem 2 
mensagens não enviadas. Sair apaga essas mensagens." with "Sair mesmo" / "Cancelar"; Cancelar keeps the session and 
both messages; Sair mesmo signs out, and after A signs in again the outbox is empty. Given 0 outbox messages, Sair 
signs out without a dialog and the presence logout behaves as before.
239:- AC-021 (FR-018): Given A is signed in on two devices (separate browser contexts) with 3 unread messages in X, 
when A reads them on device 1, then device 2's unread counter for X goes to 0 without reload, focus change, or 
reconnect.
246:- AC-028 (FR-024, BR-013): Given a send, a reconnect, and a polling episode occur, then the metrics route receives 
the corresponding events and writes structured log lines with event type, reason, and durations; a scan of those lines 
finds no message text, conversation or user names, emails, or user/conversation IDs; an unauthenticated request to the 
route is rejected and writes nothing.
247:- AC-029 (FR-025): Given the metrics route returns errors, when A sends, goes offline, and reconnects, then all 
messaging behaves as in AC-001/AC-002 and no error is shown to A.
259:## 15. Test strategy
262:  - Integration (local Supabase Postgres/Realtime and real API routes): mention validation and storage (AC-024), 
private typing-topic authorization (AC-019), `mark_conversation_read` membership (AC-030), `get_unread_counts` anon 
revoke (AC-031), create-route idempotency under replay and rate limit (AC-009, AC-038 server side), metrics route 
authentication and redaction (AC-028).
263:  - E2E (Playwright, real UI, two real accounts; plus two tabs of one account and a second device as a separate 
browser context): every other AC. Outages are simulated with browser-context offline mode and by blocking only the 
Realtime WebSocket, in both directions; lost responses with request pass-through then response drop. Expired items may 
use a test-controlled clock without changing the product rule.
269:- Mandatory failure-path tests: offline send, network error while online, lost response, two-tab drain, cancel, 
definitive rejection and recovery, rate limit, expiry, attachment offline, IndexedDB unavailable, session revoked + 
other user, sign-out with pending, short vs long Realtime drop, hidden-tab polling, polling stop, typing tab close, 
non-member typing join, forged mention, metrics route failure, restore with lost access.
282:- Non-blocking: if per-company or per-conversation breakdown of metrics is wanted later, adding identifiers to 
metric logs needs a new owner decision (BR-013 forbids them now).
